1.1.text:
Article 41
(1) The form and scope of the Integrated Service Center to realize the protection of women and children include: a. Complaint or report and protection services; b. Health and rehabilitation services;
c. Legal services;
d. Education and empowerment services;
e. Economic independence services; and
f. Spiritual services.
(2) The provision of Services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be provided to victims and/or witnesses free of charge in accordance with the laws and regulations.

Article 8
(1) Regional Government is responsible for the fulfillment of the rights of Women Victims of Discrimination, Violence, and/or Exploitation.
(2) The rights of Women Victims as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. obtaining information related to the protection of women, victim service procedures and developments in case handling;
b. reporting and having their reports followed up, providing statements, and views without pressure;
c. participating in case handling and in efforts to eliminate Violence against women; d. obtaining protection of confidentiality; e. obtaining protection for the security of themselves, their families, and their property from threats;
f. being free from all forms of stereotypes, stigma and other discrimination;
g. obtaining Services and recovery needed;
h. obtaining restitution; and
i. forming and joining organizations;
(3) The fulfillment of rights as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be carried out in a friendly and professional manner that is victim-oriented.

Article 13
(1) The Business Sector is obligated to carry out the duties and responsibilities as referred to in Article 10 covering: a. establishing company regulations that are friendly towards female workers;
b. establishing policies that prevent and protect female workers from acts of Discrimination, Violence, and/or Exploitation in the work environment;
c. providing counseling or socialization in the work environment for Prevention, protection and reporting related to acts or threats of Discrimination, Violence, and/or Exploitation against women;
d. refusing child workers under age in accordance with laws and regulations;
e. producing products that are safe for children;
f. providing corporate social responsibility for women and child protection programs;
g. providing special rooms for breastfeeding mothers or lactation rooms;
h. not involving children in company activities that endanger safety and/or are contrary to laws and regulations;
i. reporting if there are acts or threats of Discrimination, Violence, Exploitation, mistreatment, and/or neglect against women and children in the work environment;
j. providing special childcare facilities for certain companies in accordance with laws and regulations; and
k. participating in realizing Purworejo Regency as a Women-Friendly and Child-Appropriate Regency.
(2) The Business Sector implements the duties and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(3) The Business Sector that does not fulfill the obligations as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be subject to sanctions in the form of verbal warning and/or written warning.
(4) In the event that the sanctions as referred to in paragraph (3) are not implemented, action may be taken to temporarily revoke the permit and may be continued with permanent permit revocation.
(5) Permit revocation as referred to in paragraph (4) shall be carried out by the relevant Regional Apparatus in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

1.2.text:
Article 22
(1) The handling of Women Victims and Child Victims aims to provide appropriate action in handling victims and witnesses in acts or threats of Discrimination, Violence, and/or Exploitation against women and children.
(2) The handling as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. complaints or reports;
b. protection needs;
c. physical and psychological health;
d. rehabilitation;
e. Social Reintegration;
f. legal assistance; and
g. Restitution.
(3) The handling as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be carried out by the Technical Regional Apparatus and may involve related Regional Apparatus.
(4) Further provisions regarding legal assistance as referred to in paragraph (2) letter f, shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 29
(1) The Regional Government provides active and comprehensive protection to children from various actions: a. threats;
b. Violence;
c. Exploitation;
d. neglect;
e. Child Maltreatment; and
f. Injustice.
(2) Every form of threatening action as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a, whether verbal or nonverbal, that is intentionally directed at a child so as to make them feel threatened in life or safety is prohibited for any reason whatsoever.
(3) Every form of violent action as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, whether physical, psychological, or sexual violence that causes trauma to the child is prohibited for any reason whatsoever.
(4) Every form of exploitative action as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c that is carried out in any form and is not appropriate to the child's developmental age according to general views, legislation, or local wisdom and causes harm to the child is prohibited for any reason whatsoever.
(5) Every form of neglectful action as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d that disregards the responsibility as a parent, guardian, or foster parent toward the child is prohibited for any reason whatsoever.
(6) Every form of maltreatment action as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e that is carried out by exploiting closeness or abusing responsibility toward the child so as to cause physical or psychological trauma or is deemed contrary to laws and regulations is prohibited for any reason whatsoever.
(7) Every form of unjust action as referred to in paragraph (1) letter f, that is carried out by parents toward children that discriminates or shows favoritism as well as injustice in equality between males and females.

